\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Non-Gaussianity and non-generalized-freeness}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{In the coupling domain of \ref{fdip-00IC}, the state
#{\omega} of \ref{fdip-00I6} is not Gaussian. For some even
#{r\ge4}, its multilinear
\vocabk{connected correlation}{fdip-00GM}
##{(f_1,\ldots,f_r)\longmapsto
          \kappa_\omega(\Phi(f_1),\ldots,\Phi(f_r))}
is nonzero. The reconstructed Wightman field is not a generalized
free field, indeed not a Wick polynomial of a generalized free field.
In particular, its vacuum correlations are not all given by Wick
pairings of a two-point distribution.}

\proof{
\p{The hypothesis of
\citet{Corollary 4.4(1), pp. 308-309}{frohlich1974verification}
is #{\deg P>3} together with existence of the infinite-volume
interacting positive Borel cylinder measure. The corollary explicitly
names its reference [6], Section 4.1, as such a construction; that
reference is \citek{frohlich1974schwingerI}. Here
#{P(u)=\lambda u^4/24}, and \ref{fdip-00I6} identifies our
square limit with precisely that canonical half-Dirichlet state.
The proof on p. 308 obtains its quadratic exponential-integrability
input from Sections 2.2, 3.4, 3.5 and 4.1 of the HPA paper,
specifically Estimate I, Corollary 3.14(b) and the limit. Thus the
application uses the named canonical construction, without changing
the Gaussian convention.}

\p{The corollary makes this measure mutually singular to the law
of every defined Wick polynomial #{Q} of an invariant ergodic
Gaussian field. Part (1) does not impose the quasi-invariance
class used in part (2), nor the separate cluster-expansion class.
If our law were Gaussian, Euclidean invariance from
\ref{fdip-00I6} and mixing, hence ergodicity, from
\ref{fdip-00IE} would make it an admissible Gaussian comparison
law itself. Taking #{Q(u)=u} would make a probability measure
mutually singular with itself, a contradiction.}

\p{Every finite-dimensional characteristic functional is entire by
\ref{fdip-00I6}. If all cumulant forms above order two vanished,
the logarithm near the origin on each finite test span would be
quadratic. The identity theorem would then make each entire
characteristic function Gaussian, hence make the cylinder law
Gaussian. This contradicts the preceding conclusion. Odd cumulants
vanish under #{\phi\mapsto-\phi}, so some even cumulant of order
at least 4 is nonzero. This argument does not single out order 4.}

\p{Finally, the remark on
\citet{p. 310}{frohlich1974verification} identifies the associated
physical Wightman field as non-generalized-free, and more generally
not a Wick polynomial of a generalized free field. Its applicable
Euclidean construction and reconstruction are exactly those already
identified above.}
}
